From c90b27935ee4d23b9a5ccca60dc90ac5382ccb2c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: ljm-233 <2836583630@qq.com>
Date: Sat, 3 Oct 2026 15:20:57 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] screencasting: make the capture size cap configurable, and
 actually scale the frame

`cap_capture_size` was written to protect clients from a stream larger than
they can consume, but it only ever lowered the *advertised* size. Everything
else kept working on the output's size:

- the damage tracker is created from the output size in
  `dequeue_buffer_and_render`
- `render_to_shmbuf` checked the buffer against the tracker's size

So on any output above the built-in 2560x1600 bound (a 4K screen, say) the
advertised format and the allocated buffers were scaled while the render
check was not, and every frame failed with "invalid buffer size". The cap was
effectively broken above the size it was written for, which happens to be
this machine's screen, so it never showed up here.

Two changes:

1. `screencasting { max-pixels N }` replaces the hardcoded constant. The
   default is the old 2560x1600, so nothing changes unless it is set. Set it
   to 1024000 for 1280x800, 576000 for 960x600.

2. The render now scales. Rendering itself stays at the output's size -- the
   damage tracker, the element geometry and the cursor metadata are all in
   output coordinates -- and when the capture is smaller the finished frame
   is copied into a second texture with `Renderer::blit` (which takes a
   filter and returns a sync point) before it is downloaded or handed to the
   client's dmabuf. The download gets smaller as a side effect. Cursor
   metadata is scaled to match.

The limits are also read when a cast starts rather than in `PipeWire::new`,
so changing them applies to the next share instead of the next session. That
is what made the 30 Hz experiment earlier today inconclusive: the config had
changed, the running compositor had not.
